// Per-bin FLOAT SUM reduction — the order-independent-fold (reproducible
// float sum) sibling of 27-bin-fmin. TASK-0453.03 (rigour epic P3).
//
// For each input element i with integer key `key[i]` in [0, BINS) and
// float value `val[i]`, add `val[i]` into bucket `result[key[i]]`. The
// output is the per-bin sum of the values whose key selects that bin.
//
// What this example proves
// ------------------------
// That a DISTRIBUTED FLOAT SUM can carry the PRD §10.1 bit-identity
// guarantee — the gap 27-bin-fmin could not close, because IEEE-754
// addition is non-associative and plain `combine=sum` on a float
// accumulator is REJECTED (see `combine_form_for_scalar` in
// backend-common). `bin_fsum` instead declares `combine=fsum`, the
// OPT-IN reproducible float sum (TASK-0453.03): the host fan-in combines
// the per-worker partials with a plain `+` fold in a FIXED order —
// worker-id-sorted, the deterministic host event-list order every
// backend shares (TASK-0389). Because the fold order is the same on
// every backend, and each worker's partial is itself a fixed-order fold
// of its slice, every tier-1 backend produces BIT-IDENTICAL output, and
// the hand-written reference oracle reproduces the same fold (it moves in
// LOCKSTEP — see reference/src/main.rs).
//
// What `fsum` is, and is NOT (the honest residual)
// ------------------------------------------------
//   - It IS cross-backend bit-identical FOR THIS SCHEDULE: all seven
//     tier-1 backends agree, and match reference.bin, to the byte.
//   - It is NOT the naive single-pass left-to-right IEEE sum. The
//     partitioned fold `((p0 + p1) + p2) + p3` (each `pw` a per-worker
//     slice fold) associates differently, so it can round differently
//     from summing the whole array in input order. That is inherent to
//     finite float addition; `fsum` makes the chosen order REPRODUCIBLE,
//     not the arithmetic exact.
//   - It is NOT schedule-invariant: a different worker count folds the
//     partials differently and may yield different bits. The guarantee
//     is "same bits across backends for a given schedule", exactly the
//     PRD §10.1 claim — not "same bits across schedules".
//   - It covers float SUM only (f32 here). min/max were already
//     order-independent (27-bin-fmin); other float ops are out of scope.
//
// Numeric choice: f32 with fractional values (see reference README) that
// genuinely round, so the fixed fold order is LOAD-BEARING — a different
// order would give different bits. This keeps the example an honest test
// of order-determinism, not a trivially-exact integer sum in disguise.
//
// Distributed-only: this example ships a single `distributed` schedule.
// Unlike 27-bin-fmin (whose `min` combine is order-independent, so naive
// and distributed share one reference.bin), the `fsum` fold is
// schedule-specific, so a naive single-worker schedule would NOT match
// the distributed reference.bin. reference.bin is therefore the
// worker-id-sorted fold for the distributed schedule.

const N    : usize = 256;     // total input length (per stream)
const BINS : usize = 16;      // bucket count


// Integer keys select a bucket; float values are summed per bucket.
data key    : i32[N];
data val    : f32[N];
data result : f32[BINS];


// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated.
kernel load_key    : ()          -> i32[N]   effectful;
kernel load_val    : ()          -> f32[N]   effectful;
kernel save_output : (f32[BINS]) -> ()       effectful;

// Per-(element, bin) fold step. `bin_fsum(acc, k, v, bin)` adds `v` into
// `acc` iff `k == bin`, else returns `acc` unchanged. `combine=fsum`
// (TASK-0453.03) is the FLOAT-ONLY opt-in reproducible sum: under
// partition=workers each worker folds its slice into a private `result`
// (pre-init 0.0, the additive identity), and the host element-wise sums
// the per-worker partials with a `+` fold in worker-id-sorted order.
// Plain `combine=sum` on this f32 accumulator would be REJECTED.
kernel bin_fsum    : (f32, i32, f32, i32) -> f32   pure combine=fsum;


key <-- load_key();
val <-- load_val();

// `result` is pre-initialised to 0.0 (the fsum identity) by the codegen;
// each (i, b) iteration folds one value into its bucket.
for i : 0 .. N {
    for b : 0 .. BINS {
        result[b] <-- bin_fsum(result[b], key[i], val[i], b);
    }
}

save_output(result);
